course: dictionary
lesson: 9367
title: Spoof
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A spoof is a funny copy of something, such as a film, song or advert, that makes fun of it by exaggerating it. «In 1957 the BBC programme Panorama showed a spoof film of spaghetti growing on trees, and many viewers believed it.» The spoof copied a serious programme, but it was silly on purpose, so it was a joke.
explain: As a verb, to spoof something means to make that kind of funny copy. Ask yourself two questions: what is it copying, and how is it made silly? The exaggeration is the clue that it is a spoof, not the real thing.
explain: Online, spoofing is more serious. It means pretending to be someone else, for example sending an email that looks as if it comes from your bank or your school, to trick people. Informally, a spoof can also just mean a trick or a hoax.
fact: A spoof is a funny copy of something that makes fun of it by exaggerating it.
fact: «Spoof» can be a noun (a spoof) or a verb (to spoof something).
fact: Spoofs are common in films, songs and adverts, where the joke is that the copy is over the top.
fact: Online, spoofing means pretending to be someone else to trick people, such as with a fake email from your bank or school.
fact: A spoofed email, website or phone number is a warning sign of a scam: check the sender and never give out passwords.
exercise: «In 1957 the BBC programme Panorama showed a spoof film of spaghetti growing on trees.» What is a spoof here? | *A funny copy that makes fun of a serious programme | A serious news report about farming | A kind of pasta | A film about trees
exercise: To spoof a song means to: | Forget all the words | Write a new song for a school concert | Sing it very quietly | *Make a funny copy of it that exaggerates it
exercise: An email that looks as if it comes from your bank but is fake is called: | A spoof film | A birthday card | A new password | *A spoofed email
test: Which is a warning sign of an online scam? | *A spoofed email that pretends to be from your school | A reminder about a library book | A friend sharing a photo of their dog | A teacher sharing a homework list
test: In informal speech, what can a spoof also mean? | A tall tree in a park | *A trick or hoax | A bright new pair of trainers | A long journey by train
exam: Which best describes a spoof video? | *A funny copy of a video that makes fun of it by exaggerating | A serious warning from the police about scams | A video showing how to bake bread step by step | A recording of a real school assembly
